<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schmalkalden</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Schmalkalden is a town in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the south-western slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>, long known for iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> working. Shared for centuries between the counts of Henneberg and the landgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, it gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> of Protestant princes, formed there in 1531, and to Luther's Smalcald Articles of 1537.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schmalkalden.jpg" alt="Schmalkalden" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schmalkalden_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Schmalkalden lies in the district of Schmalkalden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. It stands on the southern slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>, on the Schmalkalde river, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a>. It is twinned with Fontaine in the Isère in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recklinghausen">Recklinghausen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place is first mentioned in a deed of 874 as Smalcalta, in the Frankish duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and received town privileges about 1180. When Landgrave Henry Raspe of Thuringia died without issue in 1247, it passed to the House of Henneberg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleusingen">Schleusingen</a>, while the major part of the landgraviate fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. To secure their acquisition the counts of Henneberg allied with the Landgraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and concluded an inheritance treaty. In 1360, together with Landgrave Henry II of Hesse, they paid off Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>, a son of Elisabeth of Henneberg.</p><p>In 1531 the town hall was the site where Protestant princes under the lead of Landgrave Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>, to protect their religious and political interests. In 1537 the Smalcald Articles were drawn up there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a> and other reformers. When the Henneberg line became extinct in 1583, its share was inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-landgrave-of-hesse-kassel">William IV, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel</a>, who made the town a residence and built Wilhelmsburg Castle, finished in 1590.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Lordship of Schmalkalden remained an exclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. From 1868 it was part of the Prussian province of Hesse-Nassau, until it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-saxony">Province of Saxony</a> in 1944; in 1945 it became part of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. The town was heavily damaged by bombing in the war and from 1949 formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> with Thuringia. It has its present political configuration since reunification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schmalkalden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their lands lay between Franconia and Thuringia, around Meiningen, <b>Schmalkalden</b> and Schleusingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-henneberg">County of Henneberg</a></li></ul></section>
